\section*{Significance Statement}

The UPAS-X engine represents a foundational advance in computational astrophysics, 
introducing a new class of high-precision, multi-body shadow simulation capable of 
operating at billion-point spatial scales with relativistic ephemerides and 
high-dynamic-range flux modeling. Unlike existing eclipse solvers, photometric 
pipelines, or GPU ray-tracing frameworks, UPAS-X integrates a 15th-order symplectic 
ephemeris solver, analytic ray--cone geometry, and distributed BVH spatial 
partitioning into a unified architecture that maintains numerical stability under 
extreme astrophysical conditions.

This capability enables, for the first time, real-time reconstruction of historical 
and future eclipse geometries with sub-kilometer accuracy, dense star-field 
occlusion modeling, and HDR flux attenuation across umbra, penumbra, and antumbra 
zones. The engine’s performance---exceeding 1.42 billion rays per second across 
128 NVIDIA H100 GPUs---demonstrates a scalable computational framework that 
extends beyond traditional astrophysical simulation limits.

By combining deterministic reproducibility, rigorous failure-mode monitoring, and 
cross-validation against NASA GSFC and JPL HORIZONS datasets, UPAS-X establishes 
a new methodological standard for large-scale astrophysical simulation. Its 
architecture and empirical validation framework provide a platform that can be 
extended to eclipse prediction, exoplanet transit modeling, stellar photometry, 
and multi-body dynamical systems, marking UPAS-X as a significant and field-defining 
contribution to modern computational astrophysics.